    {
      "case_id": "n01-bestow-illegal-target",
      "repeat": 1,
      "seed": 42,
      "messages": [
        {
          "role": "user",
          "content": "I cast a creature spell using its bestow cost, targeting my opponentâ€™s creature. In response, that creature leaves the battlefield. Does my spell go to the graveyard or can it still resolve?"
        }
      ],
      "card_ids": [],
      "answer": "Ruling: Your spell does **not** go to the graveyard. It ceases to be bestowed, the Aura effect ends, and it resolves as a creature spell, entering the battlefield under your control.\n\nWhy: When a bestowed Aura spell begins resolving and its target is no longer legal, it ceases to be bestowed and continues resolving as a creature spell [CR 702.103e]. Rule 608.3b explicitly carves out this case: a bestowed Aura spell with an illegal target \"becomes a creature spell and will resolve as described in rule 608.3a,\" which means it enters the battlefield under your control [CR 608.3a]. This is a specific exception to the general rule that a spell with all illegal targets is put into its owner's graveyard [CR 608.2b].",
      "sources": [
        {
          "id": "CR 702.103",
          "title": "Rule 702.103",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Bestow",
          "url": "/api/rules/702.103"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 702.103a",
          "title": "Rule 702.103a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Bestow represents a static ability that functions in any zone from which you could play the card itâ€™s on. â€œBestow [cost]â€ means â€œAs you cast this spell, you may choose to cast it bestowed. If you do, you pay [cost] rather than its mana cost.â€ Casting a spell using its bestow ability follows the rules for paying alternative costs (see 601.2b and 601.2fâ€“h).",
          "url": "/api/rules/702.103a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 702.103b",
          "title": "Rule 702.103b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "As a spell cast bestowed is put onto the stack, it becomes an Aura enchantment and gains enchant creature. It is a bestowed Aura spell, and the permanent it becomes as it resolves will be a bestowed Aura. These effects last until the spell or the permanent it becomes ceases to be bestowed (see rules 702.103eâ€“g). Because the spell is an Aura spell, its controller must choose a legal target for that spell as defined by its enchant creature ability and rule 601.2c. See also rule 303.4.",
          "url": "/api/rules/702.103b"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 702.103c",
          "title": "Rule 702.103c",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If a bestowed Aura spell is copied, the copy is also a bestowed Aura spell. Any rule that refers to a spell cast bestowed applies to the copy as well.",
          "url": "/api/rules/702.103c"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 702.103d",
          "title": "Rule 702.103d",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "When casting a spell bestowed, only its characteristics as modified by the bestow ability are evaluated to determine if it can be cast.\nExample: Aether Storm is an enchantment with the ability â€œCreature spells canâ€™t be cast.â€ This effect doesnâ€™t stop a creature card with bestow from being cast bestowed.\nExample: Garrukâ€™s Horde says, in part, â€œYou may cast creature spells from the top of your library.â€ If you control Garrukâ€™s Horde and the top card of your library is a creature card with bestow, you can cast it as a creature spell, but you canâ€™t cast it bestowed.",
          "url": "/api/rules/702.103d"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 702.103e",
          "title": "Rule 702.103e",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "As a bestowed Aura spell begins resolving, if its target is illegal, it ceases to be bestowed and the effect making it an Aura spell ends. It continues resolving as a creature spell. See rule 608.3b.",
          "url": "/api/rules/702.103e"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 702.103f",
          "title": "Rule 702.103f",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If a bestowed Aura becomes unattached, it ceases to be bestowed. If a bestowed Aura is attached to an illegal object or player, it becomes unattached and ceases to be bestowed. This is an exception to rule 704.5m.",
          "url": "/api/rules/702.103f"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 702.103g",
          "title": "Rule 702.103g",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If a bestowed Aura phases in unattached, it ceases to be bestowed. See rule 702.26, â€œPhasing.â€",
          "url": "/api/rules/702.103g"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.2b",
          "title": "Rule 608.2b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the spell or ability specifies targets, it checks whether the targets are still legal. A target thatâ€™s no longer in the zone it was in when it was targeted is illegal. Other changes to the game state may cause a target to no longer be legal; for example, its characteristics may have changed or an effect may have changed the text of the spell. If the source of an ability has left the zone it was in, its last known information is used during this process. If all its targets, for every instance of the word â€œtarget,â€ are now illegal, the spell or ability doesnâ€™t resolve. Itâ€™s removed from the stack and, if itâ€™s a spell, put into its ownerâ€™s graveyard. Otherwise, the spell or ability will resolve normally. Illegal targets, if any, wonâ€™t be affected by parts of a resolving spellâ€™s effect for which theyâ€™re illegal. Other parts of the effect for which those targets are not illegal may still affect them. If the spell or ability creates any continuous effects that affect game rules (see rule 613.11), those effects donâ€™t apply to illegal targets. If part of the effect requires information about an illegal target, it fails to determine any such information. Any part of the effect that requires that information wonâ€™t happen.\nExample: Sorinâ€™s Thirst is a black instant that reads, â€œSorinâ€™s Thirst deals 2 damage to target creature and you gain 2 life.â€ If the creature isnâ€™t a legal target during the resolution of Sorinâ€™s Thirst (say, if the creature has gained protection from black or left the battlefield), then Sorinâ€™s Thirst doesnâ€™t resolve. Its controller doesnâ€™t gain any life.\nExample: Plague Spores reads, â€œDestroy target nonblack creature and target land. They canâ€™t be regenerated.â€ Suppose the same creature land is chosen both as the nonblack creature and as the land, and the color of the creature land is changed to black before Plague Spores resolves. Plague Spores still resolves because the black creature land is still a legal target for the â€œtarget landâ€ part of the spell. The â€œdestroy target nonblack creatureâ€ part of the spell wonâ€™t affect that permanent, but the â€œdestroy target landâ€ part of the spell will still destroy it. It canâ€™t be regenerated.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.2b"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.1",
          "title": "Rule 608.1",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Each time all players pass in succession, the spell or ability on top of the stack resolves. (See rule 609, â€œEffects.â€)",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.1"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3b",
          "title": "Rule 608.3b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving has a target, it checks whether the target is still legal, as described in 608.2b. If a spell with an illegal target is a bestowed Aura spell (see rule 702.103e) or a mutating creature spell (see rule 702.140b), it becomes a creature spell and will resolve as described in rule 608.3a. Otherwise, the spell doesnâ€™t resolve. It is removed from the stack and put into its ownerâ€™s graveyard.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3b"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.2",
          "title": "Rule 608.2",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving is an instant spell, a sorcery spell, or an ability, its resolution may involve several steps. The steps described in rules 608.2a and 608.2b are followed first. The steps described in rules 608.2câ€“m are then followed as appropriate, in no specific order. The steps described in rule 608.2n and 608.2p are followed last.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.2"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3",
          "title": "Rule 608.3",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving is a permanent spell, its resolution may involve several steps. The instructions in rules 608.3a and b are always performed first. Then one of the steps in rule 608.3câ€“e is performed, if appropriate.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.2a",
          "title": "Rule 608.2a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If a triggered ability has an intervening â€œifâ€ clause, it checks whether the clauseâ€™s condition is true. If it isnâ€™t, the ability is removed from the stack and does nothing. Otherwise, it continues to resolve. See rule 603.4.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.2a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.2c",
          "title": "Rule 608.2c",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "The controller of the spell or ability follows its instructions in the order written. However, replacement effects may modify these actions. In some cases, later text on the card may modify the meaning of earlier text (for example, â€œDestroy target creature. It canâ€™t be regeneratedâ€ or â€œCounter target spell. If that spell is countered this way, put it on top of its ownerâ€™s library instead of into its ownerâ€™s graveyard.â€) Donâ€™t just apply effects step by step without thinking in these casesâ€”read the whole text and apply the rules of English to the text.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.2c"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3a",
          "title": "Rule 608.3a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving has no targets, it becomes a permanent and enters the battlefield under the control of the spellâ€™s controller.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3c",
          "title": "Rule 608.3c",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving is an Aura spell, it becomes a permanent and is put onto the battlefield under the control of the spellâ€™s controller attached to the player or object it was targeting.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3c"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3d",
          "title": "Rule 608.3d",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving is a mutating creature spell, the object representing that spell merges with the permanent it is targeting (see rule 730, â€œMerging with Permanentsâ€).",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3d"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3e",
          "title": "Rule 608.3e",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If a permanent spell resolves but its controller canâ€™t put it onto the battlefield, that player puts it into its ownerâ€™s graveyard.\nExample: Worms of the Earth has the ability â€œLands canâ€™t enter the battlefield.â€ Clone says â€œYou may have this creature enter as a copy of any creature on the battlefield.â€ If a player casts Clone and chooses to copy Dryad Arbor (a land creature) while Worms of the Earth is on the battlefield, Clone canâ€™t enter the battlefield from the stack. Itâ€™s put into its ownerâ€™s graveyard.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3e"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3f",
          "title": "Rule 608.3f",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving is a copy of a permanent spell, it will become a token permanent as it is put onto the battlefield in any of the steps above. A token put onto the battlefield this way is no longer a copy of a spell and is not â€œcreatedâ€ for the purposes of any rules or effects that refer to creating a token.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3f"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3g",
          "title": "Rule 608.3g",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving has a static ability that functions on the stack and creates a delayed triggered ability, that delayed triggered ability is created as that permanent is put onto the battlefield in any of the steps above. (See rules 702.109, â€œDash,â€ 702.152, â€œBlitz,â€ and 702.185, â€œWarp.â€)",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3g"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 405.1",
          "title": "Rule 405.1",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "When a spell is cast, the physical card is put on the stack (see rule 601.2a). When an ability is activated or triggers, it goes on top of the stack without any card associated with it (see rules 602.2a and 603.3).",
          "url": "/api/rules/405.1"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 405.2",
          "title": "Rule 405.2",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "The stack keeps track of the order that spells and/or abilities were added to it. Each time an object is put on the stack, itâ€™s put on top of all objects already there.",
          "url": "/api/rules/405.2"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 405.3",
          "title": "Rule 405.3",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If an effect puts two or more objects on the stack at the same time, those controlled by the active player are put on lowest, followed by each other playerâ€™s objects in APNAP order (see rule 101.4). If a player controls more than one of these objects, that player chooses their relative order on the stack.",
          "url": "/api/rules/405.3"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 405.5",
          "title": "Rule 405.5",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "When all players pass in succession, the top (last-added) spell or ability on the stack resolves. If the stack is empty when all players pass, the current step or phase ends and the next begins.",
          "url": "/api/rules/405.5"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 405.4",
          "title": "Rule 405.4",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Each spell has all the characteristics of the card associated with it. Each activated or triggered ability thatâ€™s on the stack has the text of the ability that created it and no other characteristics. The controller of a spell is the player who cast it. The controller of an activated ability is the player who activated it. The controller of a triggered ability is the player who controlled the abilityâ€™s source when it triggered, unless itâ€™s a delayed triggered ability. To determine the controller of a delayed triggered ability, see rules 603.7dâ€“f.",
          "url": "/api/rules/405.4"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.1",
          "title": "Rule 117.1",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Unless a spell or ability is instructing a player to take an action, which player can take actions at any given time is determined by a system of priority. The player with priority may cast spells, activate abilities, and take special actions.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.1"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.2",
          "title": "Rule 117.2",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Other kinds of abilities and actions are automatically generated or performed by the game rules, or are performed by players without receiving priority.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.2"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.7",
          "title": "Rule 117.7",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If a player with priority casts a spell or activates an activated ability while another spell or ability is already on the stack, the new spell or ability has been cast or activated â€œin response toâ€ the earlier spell or ability. The new spell or ability will resolve first. See rule 608, â€œResolving Spells and Abilities.â€",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.7"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.3",
          "title": "Rule 117.3",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Which player has priority is determined by the following rules:",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.3"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.4",
          "title": "Rule 117.4",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If all players pass in succession (that is, if all players pass without taking any actions in between passing), the spell or ability on top of the stack resolves or, if the stack is empty, the phase or step ends.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.4"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.1a",
          "title": "Rule 117.1a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A player may cast an instant spell any time they have priority. A player may cast a noninstant spell during their main phase any time they have priority and the stack is empty.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.1a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.1b",
          "title": "Rule 117.1b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A player may activate an activated ability any time they have priority.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.1b"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.1c",
          "title": "Rule 117.1c",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A player may take some special actions any time they have priority. A player may take other special actions during their main phase any time they have priority and the stack is empty. See rule 116, â€œSpecial Actions.â€",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.1c"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.1d",
          "title": "Rule 117.1d",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A player may activate a mana ability whenever they have priority, whenever they are casting a spell or activating an ability that requires a mana payment, or whenever a rule or effect asks for a mana payment (even in the middle of casting or resolving a spell or activating or resolving an ability).",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.1d"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.2a",
          "title": "Rule 117.2a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Triggered abilities can trigger at any time, including while a spell is being cast, an ability is being activated, or a spell or ability is resolving. (See rule 603, â€œHandling Triggered Abilities.â€) However, nothing actually happens at the time an ability triggers. Each time a player would receive priority, each ability that has triggered but hasnâ€™t yet been put on the stack is put on the stack. See rule 117.5.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.2a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.2b",
          "title": "Rule 117.2b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Static abilities continuously affect the game. Priority doesnâ€™t apply to them. (See rule 604, â€œHandling Static Abilities,â€ and rule 611, â€œContinuous Effects.â€)",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.2b"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.2c",
          "title": "Rule 117.2c",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Turn-based actions happen automatically when certain steps or phases begin. Theyâ€™re dealt with before a player would receive priority. See rule 117.3a. Turn-based actions also happen automatically when each step and phase ends; no player receives priority afterward. See rule 703, â€œTurn-Based Actions.â€",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.2c"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.2d",
          "title": "Rule 117.2d",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "State-based actions happen automatically when certain conditions are met. See rule 704. Theyâ€™re dealt with before a player would receive priority. See rule 117.5.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.2d"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.2e",
          "title": "Rule 117.2e",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Resolving spells and abilities may instruct players to make choices or take actions, or may allow players to activate mana abilities. Even if a player is doing so, no player has priority while a spell or ability is resolving. See rule 608, â€œResolving Spells and Abilities.â€",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.2e"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.3a",
          "title": "Rule 117.3a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "The active player receives priority at the beginning of most steps and phases, after any turn-based actions (such as drawing a card during the draw step; see rule 703) have been dealt with and abilities that trigger at the beginning of that phase or step have been put on the stack. No player receives priority during the untap step. Players usually donâ€™t get priority during the cleanup step (see rule 514.3).",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.3a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.3b",
          "title": "Rule 117.3b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "The active player receives priority after a spell or ability (other than a mana ability) resolves.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.3b"
        },
