          "text": "Unlike triggered abilities, state-based actions pay no attention to what happens during the resolution of a spell or ability.\nExample: A player controls Maro, a creature with the ability â€œMaroâ€™s power and toughness are each equal to the number of cards in your handâ€ and casts a spell whose effect is â€œDiscard your hand, then draw seven cards.â€ Maro will temporarily have toughness 0 in the middle of the spellâ€™s resolution but will be back up to toughness 7 when the spell finishes resolving. Thus Maro will survive when state-based actions are checked. In contrast, an ability that triggers when the player has no cards in hand goes on the stack after the spell resolves, because its trigger event happened during resolution.",
