          "title": "Rule 608.3e",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If a permanent spell resolves but its controller canâ€™t put it onto the battlefield, that player puts it into its ownerâ€™s graveyard.\nExample: Worms of the Earth has the ability â€œLands canâ€™t enter the battlefield.â€ Clone says â€œYou may have this creature enter as a copy of any creature on the battlefield.â€ If a player casts Clone and chooses to copy Dryad Arbor (a land creature) while Worms of the Earth is on the battlefield, Clone canâ€™t enter the battlefield from the stack. Itâ€™s put into its ownerâ€™s graveyard.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3e"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3f",
          "title": "Rule 608.3f",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving is a copy of a permanent spell, it will become a token permanent as it is put onto the battlefield in any of the steps above. A token put onto the battlefield this way is no longer a copy of a spell and is not â€œcreatedâ€ for the purposes of any rules or effects that refer to creating a token.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3f"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.2m",
          "title": "Rule 608.2m",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If an instant spell, sorcery spell, or ability that can legally resolve leaves the stack once it starts to resolve, it will continue to resolve fully.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.2m"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 704.5s",
          "title": "Rule 704.5s",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the number of lore counters on a Saga permanent with one or more chapter abilities is greater than or equal to its final chapter number and it isnâ€™t the source of a chapter ability that has triggered but not yet left the stack, that Sagaâ€™s controller sacrifices it. See rule 714, â€œSaga Cards.â€",
          "url": "/api/rules/704.5s"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 704.5r",
          "title": "Rule 704.5r",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If a permanent with an ability that says it canâ€™t have more than N counters of a certain kind on it has more than N counters of that kind on it, all but N of those counters are removed from it.",
          "url": "/api/rules/704.5r"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 704.5t",
          "title": "Rule 704.5t",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If a playerâ€™s venture marker is on the bottommost room of a dungeon card, and that dungeon card isnâ€™t the source of a room ability that has triggered but not yet left the stack, the dungeon cardâ€™s owner removes it from the game. See rule 309, â€œDungeons.â€",
          "url": "/api/rules/704.5t"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 603.1",
          "title": "Rule 603.1",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Triggered abilities have a trigger condition and an effect. They are written as â€œ[When/Whenever/At] [trigger condition or event], [effect]. [Instructions (if any).]â€",
          "url": "/api/rules/603.1"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 603.1a",
          "title": "Rule 603.1a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A triggered ability may include instructions after its effects that limit what the ability may target or state that it canâ€™t be countered. This text is not part of the abilityâ€™s effect. It functions while the ability is on the stack.",
          "url": "/api/rules/603.1a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 603.1b",
          "title": "Rule 603.1b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A triggered ability may have more than one trigger condition, and an instruction that refers to whether â€œallâ€ of those conditions have happened during a particular period. This refers to whether or not all of those conditions have occurred during that period, regardless of whether that ability has triggered based on those conditions.",
          "url": "/api/rules/603.1b"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 603.2",
          "title": "Rule 603.2",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Whenever a game event or game state matches a triggered abilityâ€™s trigger event, that ability automatically triggers. The ability doesnâ€™t do anything at this point.",
          "url": "/api/rules/603.2"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 603.3",
          "title": "Rule 603.3",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Once an ability has triggered, its controller puts it on the stack as an object thatâ€™s not a card the next time a player would receive priority. See rule 117, â€œTiming and Priority.â€ The ability becomes the topmost object on the stack. It has the text of the ability that created it, and no other characteristics. It remains on the stack until itâ€™s countered, it resolves, a rule causes it to be removed from the stack, or an effect moves it elsewhere.",
          "url": "/api/rules/603.3"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 603.3a",
          "title": "Rule 603.3a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A triggered ability is controlled by the player who controlled its source at the time it triggered, unless itâ€™s a delayed triggered ability. To determine the controller of a delayed triggered ability, see rules 603.7dâ€“f.",
          "url": "/api/rules/603.3a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 603.3b",
          "title": "Rule 603.3b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If multiple abilities have triggered since the last time a player received priority, the abilities are placed on the stack in a two-part process. First, each player, in APNAP order, puts each triggered ability they control with a trigger condition that isnâ€™t another ability triggering on the stack in any order they choose. (See rule 101.4.) Second, each player, in APNAP order, puts all remaining triggered abilities they control on the stack in any order they choose. Then the game once again checks for and performs state-based actions until none are performed, then abilities that triggered during this process go on the stack. This process repeats until no new state-based actions are performed and no abilities trigger. Then the appropriate player gets priority.",
          "url": "/api/rules/603.3b"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 603.3c",
          "title": "Rule 603.3c",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If a triggered ability is modal, its controller announces the mode choice when putting the ability on the stack. If one of the modes would be illegal (due to an inability to choose legal targets, for example), that mode canâ€™t be chosen. If no mode is chosen, the ability is removed from the stack. (See rule 700.2.)",
          "url": "/api/rules/603.3c"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 603.3d",
          "title": "Rule 603.3d",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "The remainder of the process for putting a triggered ability on the stack is identical to the process for casting a spell listed in rules 601.2câ€“d. If a choice is required when the triggered ability goes on the stack but no legal choices can be made for it, or if a rule or a continuous effect otherwise makes the ability illegal, the ability is simply removed from the stack.",
          "url": "/api/rules/603.3d"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 603.5",
          "title": "Rule 603.5",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Some triggered abilitiesâ€™ effects are optional (they contain â€œmay,â€ as in â€œAt the beginning of your upkeep, you may draw a cardâ€). These abilities go on the stack when they trigger, regardless of whether their controller intends to exercise the abilityâ€™s option or not. The choice is made when the ability resolves. Likewise, triggered abilities that have an effect â€œunlessâ€ something is true or a player chooses to do something will go on the stack normally; the â€œunlessâ€ part of the ability is dealt with when the ability resolves.",
          "url": "/api/rules/603.5"
        }
